      "id": 1,
      "text": "Poletown East got its name from the Polish immigrants who first settled there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poletown_East%2C_Detroit",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1981 General Motors used eminent domain to clear 4,200 residents, 1,300 homes, 140 businesses, six churches and a hospital for a new plant.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poletown_East%2C_Detroit",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The new Cadillac plant occupied the site of the historic Poletown plant that closed in 1980, taking a quarter of Hamtramck’s tax revenue.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.detroithistorical.org/learn/online-research/encyclopedia-of-detroit/poletown",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The neighborhood was once a bustling Polish market hub, with the Chene‑Ferry Market serving traditional favorites.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eherg.com/poletown-east",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Poletown East borders the enclave city of Hamtramck and is bounded by I‑94 to the north and I‑75 to the west.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poletown_East%2C_Detroit",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The area was once part of a 465‑acre parcel that included 1,500 homes, 144 businesses and 16 churches before the plant’s construction.",
